+\subsection{Exhaustive bounded braid audit and explicit arity-four refinements}
+
+The word ``shadow'' has two relevant meanings in the literature.
+\emph{What are GT-shadows?} uses arity four and the pentagon
+\citep{DolgushevLeLorenz2024}.
+\emph{GT-shadows for the gentle version of the
+Grothendieck--Teichmueller group} uses arity three for
+$\widehat{\mathrm{GT}}_{\rm gen}=\widehat{\mathrm{GT}}_0$
+\citep{DolgushevGuynee2024}.
+The following supplement exhausts the original braid-quotient bound,
+records the source kernel of every pair, and specifies precisely the
+arity-four targets used. A shadow is called \emph{genuine} only when
+it is the reduction of a global group element.
+
+\begin{statement}{E.14}{Proved}{All marked braid quotients through order one thousand}
+There are exactly $1946$ normal subgroups $K\triangleleft B_3$ with
+$[B_3:K]\leq1000$: $1000$ cyclic quotient kernels and $946$
+noncyclic quotient kernels. Exactly $471$ are contained in $PB_3$.
+For every noncyclic quotient $Q=B_3/K$, put
+\[
+ X=a^2,\quad Y=b^2,\quad Z=ba^2b^{-1},\quad C=(ab)^3= XZY,
+ \quad H=\langle X,Y\rangle,
+ \quad n=\operatorname{lcm}(|X|,|C|).
+\]
+The executed audit lists every pair
+\[
+ m\in\mathbb Z/n,\quad \gcd(2m+1,n)=1,
+ \qquad f\in H'
+\]
+satisfying the two braid hexagons and surjectivity on $H$.
+It obtains the following counts, all of which count pairs:
+\[
+\begin{array}{l|r}
+\text{noncyclic candidate pairs}&255167\\
+\text{both hexagons}&100055\\
+\text{both hexagons and surjectivity on }H&89721\\
+\text{among these, source kernel equals target kernel}&72251\\
+\text{gentle shadows on the }471\text{ eligible kernels}&19147.
+\end{array}
+\]
+Every surviving pair has its exact source kernel
+$L=\ker(T_{m,f}:B_3\to Q)$ recorded. It gives an isomorphism
+$B_3/L\simeq B_3/K$; it is an automorphism of the stated quotient
+only when $L=K$.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+The exhaustive normal-subgroup call is
+\[
+ \texttt{LowIndexNormalSubgroupsSearchForAll(B3,1000)}.
+\]
+We use LINS~0.9 and GAP~4.15.1. Its documented default searches every
+normal subgroup through the given index, without a restriction
+on quotient isomorphism types \citep[\S2.3.2]{Rober2024LINS}.
+The script \texttt{e\_b3\_quotient\_export.g} exports the regular
+coset permutations of both named braid generators. It checks
+normality of the pure image, the braid relation, and quotient
+orders. The authoritative completed file is
+\texttt{e\_b3\_quotients\_complete.json}; optional SmallGrp labels
+are not used to establish completeness. Canonical regular actions
+independently check that no marked kernel was duplicated.
+The cyclic count also follows from $B_3^{\rm ab}=\mathbb Z$.
+Every finite nilpotent quotient is cyclic: each Sylow quotient has
+cyclic abelianization and is cyclic by the Frattini criterion.
+Thus the large collections of unrelated prime-power groups do
+not need enumeration.
+
+The two equations evaluated by \texttt{e\_b3\_hexagons.py} are
+\begin{align*}
+ aX^m f^{-1}bY^m f&=f^{-1}ab(ZY)^m,\\
+ f^{-1}bY^m f\,aX^m&=ba(XZ)^m f.
+\end{align*}
+They are the exact braid hexagons of
+\citet[(2.18)--(2.19)]{DolgushevLeLorenz2024} and
+\citet[(3.3)--(3.4)]{DolgushevGuynee2024}.
+Since $C$ is central, $PB_3=F_2\times\langle C\rangle$ implies
+$\operatorname{im}(PB_3)'=H'$. Conjugates of $[X,Y]$ by all
+members of $H$ generate $H'$, so the associator enumeration is
+exhaustive. The integer $n$ equals the least common multiple
+of the orders of $X,Y,XZ,ZY$, as required by the shadow definition.
+
+For each pair passing the hexagons, the program separately checks
+\[
+ a'=a^{2m+1},\qquad b'=f^{-1}b^{2m+1}f,
+ \qquad a'b'a'=b'a'b',\qquad(a'b')^3=C^{2m+1}.
+\]
+It computes the subgroup generated by $X^{2m+1}$ and
+$f^{-1}Y^{2m+1}f$, and retains the pair exactly when this subgroup
+is $H$. Surjectivity on $B_3/K$ is also checked.
+For $K\subset PB_3$ these tests are precisely the gentle-shadow
+conditions; the equivalence of the three surjectivity tests is
+\citet[Propositions~3.2, 3.5 and 3.6]{DolgushevGuynee2024}.
+Finally, the regular action generated by $a',b'$ is canonically
+compared with every recorded kernel of the same index.
+All comparisons identified a source kernel in the exhaustive list.
+When that kernel equals $K$, all two-generator Cayley edges are
+checked again for descent. This explains why the larger count of
+surjective pairs must not be called a count of automorphisms.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{statement}{E.15}{Proved}{Every bounded pair is a projection of a full finite shadow on a declared refinement}
+Let $K\triangleleft B_3$ have finite index, without requiring
+$K\subset PB_3$. With $n$ as in E.14, define
+\[
+ \bar K=K\cap PB_3,\qquad
+ K^\circ=\bar K\cap
+ \ker\bigl(\ell:PB_3\longrightarrow(\mathbb Z/n)^3\bigr),
+ \qquad
+ \mathcal N_4(K)=\bigcap_{i=1}^4 d_i^{-1}(\bar K),
+\]
+where $\ell$ records the three pure linking numbers and $d_i$
+forgets strand $i$. Then $\mathcal N_4(K)\triangleleft B_4$ has
+finite index and is contained in $PB_4$. Its induced arity-three
+kernel in the Dolgushev--Le--Lorenz construction is exactly
+$K^\circ$, and
+\[
+ [B_3:K^\circ]=6|H'|n^3.
+\]
+Every pair retained in E.14 is the projection of a charming full
+GT-shadow with target $\mathcal N_4(K)$. If its original source
+kernel is $L$, its induced arity-three arrow is
+\[
+ B_3/L^\circ\ \xrightarrow{\ \sim\ }\ B_3/K^\circ.
+\]
+The resulting exhaustive projected-shadow list has $393913$
+pairs: $304192$ from the cyclic quotients and $89721$ from the
+noncyclic quotients. This counts shadows on these declared targets,
+indexed by the original marked quotient $K$ and projected to that
+quotient. Different $K$ can give the same arity-four target, so
+this is not a count after deduplicating those targets. The original
+quotients all have order at most $1000$; the list does
+not classify all arity-four targets whose induced kernel is
+exactly the original $K$.
+\end{statement}
