+$\mathcal N_4(K)$ is $B_4$-normal. Its pure quotient embeds in
+four copies of $\operatorname{im}(PB_3)$, so it is finite.
+
+Let $\varphi$ run over the five insertion/cabling maps
+$123$, $12,3,4$, $1,23,4$, $1,2,34$, $234$ of
+\citet[(A.16)--(A.18)]{DolgushevLeLorenz2024}.
+The twenty composites $d_i\varphi:PB_3\to PB_3$ have a particularly
+simple description. Each is the identity, or factors through one
+of the three linking coordinates and maps its generator to one
+of $X,Y,XZ,ZY$. For each linking coordinate all the necessary
+orders occur, with least common multiple $n$.
+These assertions follow by deleting a letter from
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|ccc}
+\varphi&X&Y&Z\\\hline
+123&x_{12}&x_{23}&x_{13}\\
+12,3,4&x_{13}x_{23}&x_{34}&x_{14}x_{24}\\
+1,23,4&x_{12}x_{13}&x_{24}x_{34}&x_{14}\\
+1,2,34&x_{12}&x_{23}x_{24}&x_{13}x_{14}\\
+234&x_{23}&x_{34}&x_{24}.
+\end{array}
+\]
+Thus membership in every $\varphi^{-1}(\mathcal N_4(K))$ means
+exactly membership in $\bar K$ and vanishing of all linking
+coordinates modulo $n$. This proves the asserted induced kernel.
+The script \texttt{e\_forgetful\_refinement.py} independently
+constructs and records all twenty composites.
+
+All three pure generators have the same order in $Q$, dividing
+$n$. Their $n$th powers belong to $\bar K$, so
+$\ell(K^\circ)=n\mathbb Z^3$. Furthermore
+\[
+ K^\circ\cap PB_3'=K\cap PB_3'.
+\]
+Consequently $(PB_3/K^\circ)^{\rm ab}=(\mathbb Z/n)^3$ and
+$(PB_3/K^\circ)'\simeq H'$, proving the index formula.
+In particular, $X$ and $C$ both have order $n$ in the refined
+quotient. The same intersection identity holds with $F_2'$ in
+place of $PB_3'$, so no new associator residue has to be chosen.
+
+Choose a representative $f\in F_2'$ of a retained associator.
+Both hexagon defects have zero pure abelianization: after passing
+to linking numbers $f$ vanishes, and conjugation merely permutes
+the three coordinates. Thus a hexagon valid modulo $K$ is valid
+modulo $K^\circ$. Let $H^\circ$ be the refined image of $F_2$.
+The map $H^\circ\to H$ has central kernel, since $H^\circ$ is a
+subgroup of $H\times(\mathbb Z/n)^2$. Let $S$ be the subgroup
+of $H^\circ$ generated by the two substituted pure generators.
+The original surjectivity gives $H^\circ=S\ker(H^\circ\to H)$,
+so $(H^\circ)'=[S,S]\subset S$. On
+$(H^\circ)^{\rm ab}=(\mathbb Z/n)^2$ the substitution is the
+invertible scalar $2m+1$. Hence $S=H^\circ$.
+
+The pentagon needs no enumeration of the possibly large product
+group. Since $f\in F_2'$, its value on two elements of a cyclic
+group is $1$. The five cabling images of $f$, after the four
+deletions, are respectively
+\[
+\begin{array}{c|ccccc|cc}
+ &123&12,3,4&1,23,4&1,2,34&234&\text{left}&\text{right}\\\hline
+ d_1&1&f&1&1&f&f&f\\
+ d_2&1&f&f&1&1&f&f\\
+ d_3&1&1&f&f&1&f&f\\
+ d_4&f&1&1&f&1&f&f.
+\end{array}
+\]
+The last columns are precisely the two pentagon products.
+Thus the pentagon holds modulo $\mathcal N_4(K)$.
+Surjectivity in arities two and three implies surjectivity in
+arity four by \citet[Proposition~2.10]{DolgushevLeLorenz2024};
+the pair is a charming full shadow for this target.
+
+The source of the refined arity-three map is $L^\circ$.
+Indeed, projecting to the original target forces membership in
+$L$; projecting to $\mathfrak S_3$ forces purity; and the pure
+abelianization is multiplied by the unit $2m+1$, so vanishing
+there is exactly the linking condition defining $L^\circ$.
+The orders defining $n$ agree for source and target because
+$X$ and $C$ map to their $(2m+1)$st powers. This proves the
+stated groupoid typing without assuming that either original
+kernel is preserved.
+
+Exactly eight original kernels are unchanged by this construction:
+\[
+\begin{array}{r|r|r|r}
+ |B_3/K|&n&|H'|&\text{charming shadows}\\\hline
+ 6&1&1&1\\48&2&1&2\\162&3&1&2\\384&4&1&4\\
+ 486&3&3&6\\648&3&4&6\\750&5&1&4\\768&4&2&4.
+\end{array}
+\]
+For $K\subset PB_3$, the unchanged-kernel criterion can also be
+written $|\operatorname{im}(PB_3)^{\rm ab}|=|X|^3$ and
+$|C|=|X|$. The table contains $29$ shadows. Across all $1946$
+original quotients, $40$ refinements still have order at most
+$1000$; the largest refined order is $5982017994$.
+These orders are computed from the formula, not by enumerating
+those large groups.
+
+Finally, this construction does not establish genuineness.
+Every target $\mathcal N_4(K)$ contains the Brunnian subgroup
+$\bigcap_i\ker d_i$. The selected arity-four targets therefore
+lose the constraints detected on that subgroup. Global
+genuineness requires survival through every further finite
+refinement, as in \citet[Corollary~3.13]{DolgushevLeLorenz2024}.
+\end{proof}
+
+\begin{statement}{E.16}{Proved}{Exact class-two images and an explicit finite fake shadow}
+Whenever the finite group $H$ in E.14 has nilpotency class at
+most two, put $u=[X,Y]$ and $d=|u|$. The exact reductions of
+$\widehat{\mathrm{GT}}$, of $\widehat{\mathrm{GT}}_0$, and of
+$G_{\mathbb Q}$ to the pair data $(m,f)$ on this quotient are
+all the same set:
+\[
+ \left\{\left(\frac{\chi-1}{2}\bmod n,
+        u^{(\chi^2-1)/24}\right):
+ \chi\in(\mathbb Z/M)^\times\right\},
+ \qquad M=\operatorname{lcm}(2n,24d).
+\]
+Thus complete genuine images are determined for all $1000$
+cyclic and $354$ noncyclic quotients in the enumeration.
+The class-two computation also proves that $180$ retained
+finite-shadow pairs, on ten marked kernels of orders $486$
+and $972$, are not genuine. In particular there is an explicit
+gentle shadow with original quotient order $486$ which is not
+the reduction of any global $\widehat{\mathrm{GT}}_0$ element.
+Its E.15 refinement is a charming full finite GT-shadow,
+also fake, with induced braid quotient order $13122$.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+The coefficient must first be computed in the torsion-free
+free profinite nilpotent group of class two. Write its commutator
+as $u$ and the associator as $u^r$, with $r\in\widehat{\mathbb Z}$.
+The simplified hexagon of
+\citet[Proposition~3.4]{DolgushevGuynee2024}, with
+$z=(XY)^{-1}$, gives
+\[
+ X^m f(z,X)z^m f(Y,z)Y^m f(X,Y)=1.
+\]
+Each substituted associator is $u^r$, while
+$X^m z^mY^m=u^{-m(m+1)/2}$. Thus
+\[
+ 3r=\frac{m(m+1)}2,
+ \qquad 24r=(2m+1)^2-1=\chi^2-1
+ \quad\hbox{in }\widehat{\mathbb Z}.
+\]
+Multiplication by $3$ is injective here. The expression
+$(\chi^2-1)/24$ is integral for every profinite unit $\chi$,
+and reducing it modulo $d$ is determined by $\chi$ modulo
+$24d$. Every finite class-two image of $F_2'$ is generated by
+$u$, so this proves necessity of the displayed list.
+Cyclotomic surjectivity realizes every unit residue in that
+list by an actual Galois element, proving sufficiency and the
+claimed equality of the three images. This argument does not
+identify $G_{\mathbb Q}$ with either global GT group.
+
+The program evaluates this formula in every applicable quotient
+and compares it with all finite-shadow pairs. It obtains
+\[
+\begin{array}{r|r|r|r|r}
+\text{quotient order}&\text{marked kernels}&
+ \text{pairs per kernel}&\text{genuine per kernel}&
+ \text{fake per kernel}\\\hline
+486&5&18&6&12\\972&5&36&12&24.
+\end{array}
+\]
+In all these rows the kernel is contained in $PB_3$.
+For other noncyclic quotients, identity and complex conjugation,
+and any unique survivor at a fixed $m$, are independently genuine
+by cyclotomic surjectivity. In total the bounded audit certifies
+$15719$ genuine noncyclic pairs. The complete genuine image in
+the remaining $592$ noncyclic quotients is not determined by
+these tests.
+
+Here is a standalone certificate for the first fake row.
+Take the following permutations, with the right-action convention:
+\begingroup\small
+\begin{align*}
+ a={}&(1\ 2\ 6\ 17\ 34\ 49\ 44\ 27\ 14\ 32\ 42\ 24\ 37\ 50\ 43\ 25\ 9\ 3)\\
+ & (4\ 12\ 29\ 46\ 51\ 40\ 53\ 48\ 33\ 16\ 5\ 15\ 19\ 36\ 18\ 35\ 30\ 13)\\
+ & (7\ 20\ 38\ 52\ 47\ 31\ 45\ 54\ 41\ 23\ 8\ 22\ 39\ 28\ 11\ 26\ 10\ 21),\\
+ b={}&(1\ 4\ 14\ 29\ 34\ 30\ 44\ 53\ 43\ 33\ 42\ 51\ 37\ 19\ 6\ 18\ 9\ 5)\\
+ & (2\ 7\ 17\ 11\ 3\ 10\ 27\ 45\ 32\ 38\ 49\ 47\ 50\ 39\ 25\ 41\ 24\ 8)\\
+ & (12\ 20\ 35\ 26\ 13\ 31\ 48\ 54\ 46\ 52\ 40\ 22\ 36\ 28\ 16\ 23\ 15\ 21).
+\end{align*}
+\endgroup
+Direct finite multiplication verifies
+\[
+ |\langle a,b\rangle|=486,\quad aba=bab,\quad
+ |H|=81,\quad H'=\langle u\rangle\simeq C_3,
+ \quad [u,H]=1,
+\]
+\[
+ |X|=|Y|=9,\qquad Y^3=X^3=C,
+ \qquad aua^{-1}=bub^{-1}=u^{-1}.
+\]
+These checks are made by \texttt{e\_fake\_shadow.py}, which
+independently enumerates the group from the displayed
+permutations and does not use LINS or SmallGrp. The pure image
+is $H$ of index six, hence the kernel is contained in $PB_3$;
+$n=9$.
+
+Set $m=0$ and $f=u$. Since $u^3=1$ and both braid generators
+invert $u$, the two hexagons read
+\[
+ a u^{-1}bu=u^{-1}ab,
+ \qquad u^{-1}bu\,a=ba\,u,
+\]
+and hold directly. The substitution fixes $X,Y$, hence is onto
+$H$; it induces the identity on the symmetric quotient and is
+onto the whole group. Every Cayley edge verifies that it is
+an automorphism of this particular quotient.
+But a global lift would have $m\in9\widehat{\mathbb Z}$, hence
+$\chi\equiv1\pmod {18}$. The torsion-free coefficient identity
+then forces $r\equiv0\pmod3$, whereas $f=u$ has $r\equiv1$.
+This contradiction proves that the finite gentle shadow is fake.
+Division by $3$ has been performed before reduction, not in the
+finite group of exponent divisible by $3$.
+
+Applying E.15 gives a full arity-four shadow with the same
+nonzero commutator residue and with
+$[B_3:K^\circ]=6\cdot3\cdot9^3=13122$.
+For this explicit example, a separate GAP calculation constructs
+the four forgetful permutation representations, verifies the
+pentagon itself, and computes
+\[
+ |PB_4/\mathcal N_4(K)|=1594323=3^{13},
+ \qquad |PB_3/K^\circ|=2187=3^7.
+\]
+The six permutations and all five cabling maps are constructed
+in \texttt{e\_fake\_shadow\_arity4.g}. The associator residue
+and $n=9$ are unchanged, so the same coefficient obstruction
+proves that this charming full shadow is fake as well.
+\end{proof}
