+\begin{statement}{F.15}{Proved}{Continuous section classes and inverse limits}
+Let $G$ be a topological group, let $(P_n,q_n)_{n\ge1}$ be an
+inverse sequence of topological groups with continuous $G$-actions
+and continuous equivariant surjections
+$q_n:P_{n+1}\twoheadrightarrow P_n$, and give
+$P=\varprojlim_nP_n$ its inverse-limit topology.
+The natural map of pointed sets
+\[
+ H^1_{\rm cts}(G,P)\longrightarrow
+       \varprojlim_n H^1_{\rm cts}(G,P_n)                 \tag{F.15}
+\]
+is bijective in either of the following cases:
+\begin{enumerate}
+\item Every $P_n$ is compact Hausdorff. In particular this applies
+to a pro-$\ell$ group and its closed lower-central quotients.
+The nonabelian $\varprojlim{}^1$ of the associated twisted
+$H^0$ groups is a singleton, without requiring surjectivity
+on those fixed groups.
+\item For a field $k=\mathbb Q_\ell$, each
+$P_n=\exp(\mathfrak p_n)$ is the BCH group of a finite-dimensional
+nilpotent $k$-Lie algebra. The transition maps come from
+surjective $k$-Lie homomorphisms, and $G$ acts continuously
+by $k$-linear Lie automorphisms. This includes the rational
+unipotent Malcev towers used here. In this case twisted
+fixed groups have the Mittag--Leffler property, although
+the coefficient groups need not be compact.
+\end{enumerate}
+Surjectivity of (F.15) in fact holds without either additional
+condition. Injectivity can fail for noncompact pro-discrete
+coefficient groups with surjective transition maps.
+\end{statement}
+\begin{proof}
+We use the convention of F.1:
+$c^u(g)=u^{-1}c(g)g(u)$ for the conjugate of a cocycle.
+Given a compatible sequence of classes, choose a continuous
+representative $c_1$. Suppose $c_n$ has been chosen. A representative
+$b_{n+1}$ of the next class satisfies
+$q_n b_{n+1}=c_n^{a_n}$ for some $a_n\in P_n$.
+Choose a lift $\widetilde a_n\in P_{n+1}$ and put
+$c_{n+1}=b_{n+1}^{\widetilde a_n^{-1}}$. Then
+$q_nc_{n+1}=c_n$ exactly. Constant conjugation preserves
+continuity. The map $c(g)=(c_n(g))_n$ is continuous because each
+coordinate is continuous and $P$ has the inverse-limit topology;
+its cocycle identity holds coordinatewise. This proves surjectivity
+using only the stated surjections, with no continuous choice of
+lifts of varying group elements required.
+
+For injectivity, let $c,d:G\to P$ be continuous cocycles whose
+classes agree at every level. Put
+\[
+ X_n=\{u\in P_n:d_n(g)=u^{-1}c_n(g)g(u)
+                         \text{ for every }g\in G\}.
+\]
+These sets are nonempty, and $q_n(X_{n+1})\subset X_n$.
+In the compact case each $X_n$ is closed in $P_n$: for each fixed
+$g$, its defining equality is a closed condition in a Hausdorff
+group. Thus $\prod_nX_n$ is compact. The conditions
+$q_n(u_{n+1})=u_n$ are closed and have the finite-intersection
+property. Indeed any finite set of them is satisfied by choosing
+an element at a sufficiently high level and projecting it
+downwards, with arbitrary choices in the unused coordinates.
+Their common intersection is nonempty. The resulting
+$u=(u_n)_n\in P$ gives $d=c^u$ and proves injectivity.
+
+This argument also proves the stated $\varprojlim{}^1$ assertion
+directly. For any inverse sequence of compact Hausdorff groups
+$(H_n,f_n)$ and any $(a_n)\in\prod_nH_n$, the equations
+\[
+                    a_n=h_n f_n(h_{n+1})^{-1}
+\]
+have a solution: every finite initial set can be solved backwards,
+and compactness gives a simultaneous solution. These equations
+say precisely that every sequence represents the neutral class
+in nonabelian $\varprojlim{}^1$. Here the relevant groups are
+$H_n=P_n^{G,c_n}$ for the twisted action
+$g*_cu=c_n(g)g(u)c_n(g)^{-1}$. They are closed, hence compact;
+their transition maps need not be surjective.
+
+A pro-$\ell$ group is the inverse limit of its closed
+lower-central quotients. The intersection of those closed terms
+is trivial because every finite $\ell$-group quotient is
+nilpotent. Surjectivity onto their inverse limit follows from
+compactness of the nested compatible cosets. Their characteristic
+definition ensures equivariance for any continuous arithmetic
+action. This justifies the pro-$\ell$ specialization in F.12;
+it does not identify a full profinite group with its nilpotent
+quotient tower.
+
+For the BCH case, retain the nonempty sets $X_n$. The twisted
+action differentiates to the $k$-linear Lie automorphisms
+\[
+       v\longmapsto\operatorname{Ad}(c_n(g))\,g(v)
+                          \quad(v\in\mathfrak p_n).
+\]
+Exponential is a functorial bijection in the BCH construction.
+Consequently
+$H_n=P_n^{G,c_n}=\exp(\mathfrak h_n)$, where
+$\mathfrak h_n$ is the common fixed linear subspace of these
+automorphisms. For fixed $n$, the images of
+$\mathfrak h_m\to\mathfrak h_n$ form a descending sequence
+of finite-dimensional linear subspaces and therefore stabilize.
+The point-group images $H_m\to H_n$ are exactly their
+exponentials, so they stabilize as well; no compactness of
+$k$-points is being assumed.
+
+The set $X_n$ is a left torsor under $H_n$. Its images from
+higher levels are nested nonempty cosets of the corresponding
+images of $H_m$. Once those subgroups stabilize, the nested
+cosets stabilize too. Thus $(X_n)$ is a Mittag--Leffler sequence
+of nonempty sets. For completeness, put $Y_n$ equal to its
+eventual image at level $n$. The maps
+$Y_{n+1}\to Y_n$ are surjective: choose a level high enough
+that both images have stabilized, and lift any prescribed
+element of $Y_n$ from that level. Recursive choices in these
+nonempty surjective sets give an element of $\varprojlim X_n$.
+It again conjugates $c$ to $d$. This proves injectivity and
+explains precisely why finite dimensionality replaces compactness
+for the rational towers.
+
+Finally the noncompact distinction is real. Take the profinite
+group $G=(\mathbb Z_2,+)$, the discrete abelian groups
+\[
+ P_n=\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z/2^n,
+ \qquad t\cdot(a,b)=(a,b+ta\bmod 2^n),
+\]
+and transition maps given by reduction of the second coordinate.
+These actions are continuous and the transitions are surjective.
+A continuous cocycle has zero first coordinate: it would otherwise
+give a nonzero continuous homomorphism from the compact group
+$\mathbb Z_2$ to the torsion-free discrete group $\mathbb Z$.
+Its second coordinate is therefore a continuous homomorphism
+$\mathbb Z_2\to\mathbb Z/2^n$. Every such homomorphism is a
+coboundary, since an integer $a$ can represent any residue
+modulo $2^n$. Thus $H^1_{\rm cts}(G,P_n)=0$ for all $n$.
+But $P=\mathbb Z_{\rm disc}\oplus\mathbb Z_2$, and the same
+calculation gives
+\[
+               H^1_{\rm cts}(G,P)=\mathbb Z_2/\mathbb Z.
+\]
+For example $t\mapsto(0,t/3)$ represents a nonzero class
+trivial at every finite level. The fixed groups
+$P_n^G=2^n\mathbb Z\oplus\mathbb Z/2^n$ have nonstabilizing
+images from higher levels. The program \texttt{g\_f\_limit.py}
+checks the coherent residues of $1/3$ and their finite
+coboundary formulas through $n=12$, and all cocycle pairs
+through $n=8$. It does not claim that the integer conjugators
+are compatible; their failure to be compatible is precisely
+the counterexample proved above. This counterexample does not assert
+failure for the particular arithmetic realization
+$\mathcal V_{\rm ar}$; it shows why its topology and fixed-group
+condition must be checked separately.
+\end{proof}
